# Ask whether an epistemic formula holds in the current state.
#
# This is what a knowledge precondition and an epistemic goal both reduce to.
# The formula is written the way the policy carries it — "(K r1 (clear
# corridor))", "(Kw A tails)" — over the vocabulary of the loaded task.
string formula

# Whose point of view to evaluate it from. Empty, the default, is the model as
# it stands: what is actually the case, and what the agents therefore know.
#
# Naming an agent evaluates in that agent's perspective instead — the worlds it
# considers possible from the ones that are actual. The two answer different
# questions. "Is the corridor blocked" is about the world; "would r2 say the
# corridor is blocked" is about r2, and an agent that has not looked has no
# opinion either way even when the fact is settled.
#
# This is what makes a false belief expressible: under KD45 an agent's
# perspective can exclude the actual world, and a formula can hold there while
# failing in the model.
string agent
---
bool success       # false when no task is loaded or the formula does not parse
bool holds         # meaningful only when success is true
string error
